Method Stack:
------------
// User Defined Checked Exception
// Since it extends Exception, it becomes a Checked Exception
class InvalidNumberException extends Exception {
    // Constructor
    InvalidNumberException(String msg) {
        // Calling parent Exception constructor
        // to store custom error message
        super(msg);
    }
}

public class Demo {
    // Method-2
    // Declares that it may throw InvalidNumberException
    void m2(int a, int b) throws InvalidNumberException {
        System.out.println("m2() started");
        try {
            // Risky code
            // If b = 0, ArithmeticException occurs
            int c = a / b;
            System.out.println("Result = " + c);

            // Business Validation
            // Throw user-defined exception if result is negative
            if (c < 0) {
                throw new InvalidNumberException(
                        "Negative value not allowed");
            }
        }
        catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println(
                    "Arithmetic Exception Occurred");
            // Rethrowing exception
            // Send exception to caller method
            throw e;
        }
        finally {
            // Executes whether exception occurs or not
            System.out.println(
                    "m2() finally block executed");
        }
        // Executes only if no exception occurs
        System.out.println("m2() ended");
    }

    // Method-1
    // Again propagating exception to caller
    void m1(int a, int b)
            throws InvalidNumberException {
        System.out.println("m1() started");
        // Calling m2()
        m2(a, b);
        // Executes only if m2() completes successfully
        System.out.println("m1() ended");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("main() started");
        try {
            Demo d = new Demo();
            // Calling m1()
            d.m1(10, 0);
        }

        // Handles User Defined Exception
        catch (InvalidNumberException e) {
            System.out.println(
                    "User Defined Exception : "
                            + e.getMessage());
        }
        // Handles all remaining exceptions
        catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(
                    "Exception Handled In Main");
            // Prints complete stack trace
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
        System.out.println("main() ended");
    }
}

Exception Propagation means:
When a method does not handle an exception, JVM automatically passes (propagates) the exception to its caller method until some method handles it or the program terminates.
Ex:
java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero
    at Demo.m2(Demo.java:20)
    at Demo.m1(Demo.java:48)
    at Demo.main(Demo.java:67)
┌─────────────┐
│   main()    │
├─────────────┤
│    m1()     │
├─────────────┤
│    m2()     │
├─────────────┤
│   10 / 0    │  ← Exception Occurs Here
└─────────────┘

Exception travels upward:

m2()  →  m1()  →  main()

main()
  │
  ├── calls m1()
  │
  ├── m1() calls m2()
  │
  ├── m2() executes 10/0    //Uncached exception, so need to handle with try catch block.
  │
  ├── ArithmeticException occurs
  │
  ├── catch block catches it
  │
  ├── throw e; rethrows exception
  │
  ├── finally block executes
  │
  ├── Exception goes back to m1()
  │
  ├── m1() doesn't handle it
  │
  ├── Exception goes back to main()
  │
  ├── main() catches exception
  │
  ├── printStackTrace() prints stack information
  │
  └── Program continues and ends normally

Note: Instead of e.printStackTrace() --> System.err.println("Exception occurred"+e); --> Throwable class contains toString() --> overridden method.
      Also insted of e, we can use e.getMessage()
	  
