Variable Arguments/Var-Args: Variable Arguments (Var-Args) allow a method to accept any number of arguments of the same type.
---------------------------
No lengthy class with so many methods.
Introduced In: Java 5
Syntax: datatype... variableName     --> we can call method with 2, 3, 4 .... parameters
        ... is called elipses        --> only 3 ellipses are allowed. 
		
ex:
void m1(int... a)  ----> Compiler treats it as: void m1(int[] a) --> Var-Args are internally converted into arrays.

Meaning:
The method can accept:
m1();
m1(10);
m1(10,20);
m1(10,20,30);
m1(10,20,30,40);
All are valid.

Rule 1: Only one var-arg parameter allowed.
        void m1(int... a)              //Valid:
        void m1(int... a, int... b)    //Compile Time Error

Rule 2: Var-arg must be last parameter.
        void m1(int id, String name, int... marks)    //Valid
        void m1(int... marks, String name)            //Invalid  --> Compile Time Error

Rule 3: Can mix normal parameters with var-args.
        void m1(String name, int... marks)

Note: In main() --> String[] args == String... args == String args[]

-Wrapper Classes:
-----------------
-Java is an Object-Oriented Programming Language. Everything is represented in object format.
-Primitive data types are not objects. To represent primitive values as objects, Java provides Wrapper Classes.

