TYPE CASTING: Converting one data type into another data type is called Type Casting.
-------------
Types:
1. Widening (Up Casting)
2. Narrowing (Down Casting)

WIDENING (UP CASTING): Converting lower data type into higher data type.
----------------------

Features:
---------
1. Automatic Conversion
2. No Explicit Cast Required
3. No Data Loss

Example:
---------
byte b = 20;

int i = b;

System.out.println(i);

Output:
-------
20

More Examples:
--------------
int i = 100;

long l = i;

float f = l;

double d = f;


Widening Flow:
--------------
byte → short → int → long → float → double


=========================================================
NARROWING (DOWN CASTING)
---------------------------------------------------------
Definition:
Converting higher data type into lower data type.

Features:
---------
1. Explicit Cast Required
2. Manual Conversion
3. Data Loss Possible

Example:
---------
double d = 100.75;

int i = (int)d;

System.out.println(i);

Output:
-------
100

Decimal value lost.

Example:
---------
long l = 100;

short s = (short) l;

Without Cast:
-------------
short s = l;      // Compile Time Error


Narrowing Flow:
---------------
double → float → long → int → short → byte


=========================================================
DATA LOSS EXAMPLE
---------------------------------------------------------

int i = 130;

byte b = (byte)i;

System.out.println(b);

Output:
-------
-126

Reason:
-------
Byte range = -128 to 127

Overflow occurs.


